## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to extend bit manipulation techniques to multi-register data structures while maintaining branchless performance.

## Key Concepts
- Two's complement negation for 64-bit words
- Isolating least significant set bit with x & -x
- Branchless masking using arithmetic on boolean predicates

## Code Example
```javascript
void isolateLeastBit128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_lsb = lo & -lo;
  uint64_t hi_lsb = hi & -hi;
  uint64_t nonzero_lo = -(lo != 0);
  uint64_t zero_lo = -(lo == 0);
  *out_lo = lo_lsb & nonzero_lo;
  *out_hi = hi_lsb & zero_lo;
}
```

## Example
Input: hi=0x8000, lo=0x0C00 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0400
Input: hi=0x0000, lo=0x0000 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0000

## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying known bit-hacks to multi-lane data without branching, simulating performance-critical kernels.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting across lanes
- 128-bit to four 32-bit lanes mapping
- Branchless arithmetic
- Endianness and lane indexing

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}

uint32_t popcountPerLane128(uint128 x) {
  uint32_t lo0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t lo1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t hi0 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t hi1 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);

  uint32_t p0 = popcount32(lo0);
  uint32_t p1 = popcount32(lo1);
  uint32_t p2 = popcount32(hi0);
  uint32_t p3 = popcount32(hi1);

  return (p3 << 24) | (p2 << 16) | (p1 << 8) | p0;
}
```

